#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1f374865-c7f7-510c-b361-fbad352d86c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3452" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve  $\\mathbf r(t) =  t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin t\\, \\mathbf j$ at $t = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "2ff129e7-a923-5510-a35b-a6be403152ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-3453" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {|y''(x)|} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6x|}{{(1+9{x}^{4})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)|}{{(1+9(-1)^{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6|}{{(1+9)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{10}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d8e470ad-6a9a-5a03-a90e-b21b24cb6862" :question/math-academy-id "e-3454" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{ (1+\\cos^2 t)^3 } }, \\quad t\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0927d8a-287b-54d7-8453-1b8762f3b287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(1+{t}^{2}+{t}^{4})}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360485} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b23674-bf2a-5d7d-8be7-8efeb0b3346d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2}+4t)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360486} {:answer/id #uuid "9881ef89-bffe-52bc-98b1-2e975109141e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{4})}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360487} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7d0b0-9af6-52e8-9396-6b48417925ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360488} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad4766b-ad92-5cda-a884-564b6a2fda18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(2t+{t}^{4})}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7d0b0-9af6-52e8-9396-6b48417925ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360488} :answer-field/id #uuid "f20d1866-ad62-5792-a608-3b8f787fdf2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c665a10-c572-5402-ab7b-5950f84379a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162387" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=-t\\,i+({t}^{2}+1)\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2}{}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 0|}{{((-1)^{2}+(2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2|}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab8076a-8db4-55ef-8c35-4275764f5877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360492} {:answer/id #uuid "24dc4737-18c4-51be-9132-fdd6e898c958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{(2{e}^{t}+4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360493} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4e130c-d245-583a-9a65-7ae375d21855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360494} {:answer/id #uuid "83c38e27-5ba3-529e-93eb-c4f6cccf291e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360495} {:answer/id #uuid "a7317bee-4253-5b37-b15a-e40325eb2e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7317bee-4253-5b37-b15a-e40325eb2e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360496} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9f67dc-8eec-5896-b1ba-e58f7fa904cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d67f4c8-d50b-5861-938e-920000d60dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-162388" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+2t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+{2}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2{e}^{t}|}{{({e}^{2t}+4)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c571bc2-35c7-570d-a288-b1a921ab5fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360499} {:answer/id #uuid "73de1ff4-ee8d-51cf-91a8-0c042cc2db9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7866a8d-9be9-5327-b333-d494ad8459d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501} {:answer/id #uuid "880393eb-753a-546c-9dc4-dfe6e16927c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502} {:answer/id #uuid "d81299ca-c205-557f-92d3-4842f71314a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7866a8d-9be9-5327-b333-d494ad8459d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501} :answer-field/id #uuid "5012f3a7-3b63-5e7e-b09a-8167a80cfe4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e5c10d-6a90-5598-9eca-de58bdca7cbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-162403" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{2t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}|}{{((2{e}^{2t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{t}}{{(4{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{6{e}^{(0)}}{{(4{e}^{4(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "480d11da-0304-5f72-9e44-cc11c21f408d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360506} {:answer/id #uuid "acd2afb4-791a-5273-9ec8-1489af6925f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360507} {:answer/id #uuid "a6088312-994d-5ff9-aa95-62aa9cbed6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({sin}^{3}⁡t+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360508} {:answer/id #uuid "4120ca89-3312-54b6-a72e-5a2667153757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360509} {:answer/id #uuid "4216f520-5c3b-51cc-b477-b6f8062a8454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "480d11da-0304-5f72-9e44-cc11c21f408d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360506} :answer-field/id #uuid "f678ce1b-c920-5d24-ac8e-31117f181720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5ac123c-3663-55fc-a172-6e4b0415bb6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162414" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{},\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 0-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 1|}{{((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|}{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because cosine is positive for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432453a7-04a6-5db9-9b15-158a97e066b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360513} {:answer/id #uuid "153ba1ed-f753-568a-aa19-42467ed2f998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360514} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c8e624-13a2-53ac-b199-d1630e743323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360515} {:answer/id #uuid "c221d7e7-f01e-5b7f-bb68-27bf6b9a8edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360516} {:answer/id #uuid "0461dd71-d8cd-5ecf-ab88-b96bb43e7e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c221d7e7-f01e-5b7f-bb68-27bf6b9a8edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360516} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3534edc-1576-5295-90d1-b7cc5e0bf56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9e9607-a2dd-5594-94f3-d6c7ba9a234a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162418" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{t}^{2}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2\\cdot (\\,\\,)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(t)=-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2)-(-2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e86dbb41-3190-5cb1-a2b5-14132394dd1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360520} {:answer/id #uuid "bcae384f-f753-5504-8ca1-7997b4f47cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360521} {:answer/id #uuid "8cab9783-92c5-58db-8e0b-aa3265f50d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360522} {:answer/id #uuid "0821b046-a964-5bff-855e-605d4f6050ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360523} {:answer/id #uuid "f3019d67-05d3-589c-8b07-d2b4cb5298cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcae384f-f753-5504-8ca1-7997b4f47cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360521} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f3ce2a-308f-5c39-9f74-d69b72339b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2299202-b673-50d0-ad04-bb6f2463ec0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162420" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{}{\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{3}}},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-{t}^{-1},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={t}^{-2},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{-{t}^{-1}\\cdot 0-1\\cdot ({t}^{-2})}{{((-{t}^{-1})^{2}+(1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}}{{({t}^{-2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot ({t}^{2})^{3/2}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot |t{|}^{3}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot {t}^{3}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-t}{\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79277aa-cbb1-57a1-82d5-8f0b893e94cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360527} {:answer/id #uuid "c112bad1-a558-5110-8023-22629abc9616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360528} {:answer/id #uuid "53a23580-64aa-5fd0-bf2a-81839395371c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360529} {:answer/id #uuid "88c5cf84-4fcc-577e-9aa5-31603bc7e1d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360530} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ae1406-5c53-5a6e-8f7b-7591ebd60af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a79277aa-cbb1-57a1-82d5-8f0b893e94cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360527} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b2edb53-5676-5944-8e6f-a77440c922d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfea61b1-f938-5b31-b11a-e78fec630194" :question/math-academy-id "q-162424" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=2{e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{t}\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{t}|}{{((2{e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{3t}}{{(4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82ae02f7-0298-50d4-a513-51cb9242ed9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360534} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2aa8a5-1c0c-5388-88f3-25c86fe18f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360535} {:answer/id #uuid "06d26735-fa5b-5eaa-a562-a743b4e657df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360536} {:answer/id #uuid "37de2f4d-2f1c-5289-91ba-51fd2dfbbb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360537} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd0ac98-f093-52d7-a6db-7d23320db589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2aa8a5-1c0c-5388-88f3-25c86fe18f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360535} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a0c862-4efc-5e93-a559-abbeab4a0c14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36ebf6b1-3a18-5cf6-aafc-2c0ec5515fc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162425" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨(1+t)^{2},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1+t)^{2},\\,y(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2(1+t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2(1+t)\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 2|}{{(\\,(2(1+t))^{2}+(2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8{((1+t)^{2}+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{1}{2{((1+(0))^{2}+(0)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1+0)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61abafab-ce6c-55a8-b6fd-2767452be3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360541} {:answer/id #uuid "f529ffec-33ce-5afb-8a43-8a3c8f533018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360542} {:answer/id #uuid "319c3b07-85f2-587f-9bf9-97e1c1671330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360543} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccbea95-33d3-5c7c-8375-22ef41db6eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360544} {:answer/id #uuid "2b47c0e9-9c9d-51b0-9076-ac97acfcb0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b47c0e9-9c9d-51b0-9076-ac97acfcb0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba2176e-9581-52ed-acf9-4d337f3307b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "335f364d-6d48-59d7-8861-1972f730d325" :question/math-academy-id "q-162476" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-8{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf25ff9-66dd-5a44-a7d4-031d13f59985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360548} {:answer/id #uuid "88b39bc6-1287-552e-b95f-04e82975189d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360549} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb98ae0-e0fc-5ccc-a358-0b0fda139f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360550} {:answer/id #uuid "efae2038-ef4c-57ef-889a-527e252ffc29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360551} {:answer/id #uuid "1c22991a-9f29-5d82-9d67-847a2134b7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bf25ff9-66dd-5a44-a7d4-031d13f59985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360548} :answer-field/id #uuid "64121242-e881-539d-8b78-0a477dae4d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e448f92d-acb3-51b2-99d6-b710840c0ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162525" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-4x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x-4|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)-4|}{{(1+{(3(-1)^{2}-4(-1))}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10|}{{(1+{7}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{(50)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{50\\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e4cadd-d138-5493-9838-7eccadbf0a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360555} {:answer/id #uuid "e7555779-dd38-500d-9342-951c89bdbb45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360556} {:answer/id #uuid "608170bc-1ad3-5ced-8bfd-0c39f1c40498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360557} {:answer/id #uuid "20af2c31-0df2-581a-8aa5-69048bac977e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360558} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2b3198-fe9f-58db-a0db-d01366774920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55e4cadd-d138-5493-9838-7eccadbf0a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360555} :answer-field/id #uuid "e65c04fe-056c-559e-831f-62a5b6a593e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7692858e-e7c4-5025-bcf9-d2dc03758f14" :question/math-academy-id "q-162526" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0|}{{(1+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7820ce4-ec69-55d5-b3c5-78a68055b77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360562} {:answer/id #uuid "9794adc2-8663-5aa4-a0cf-8623185dc810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360563} {:answer/id #uuid "2def3a6b-9d19-58a7-9867-721ac3f0839d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360564} {:answer/id #uuid "ce26222c-742b-5a86-8122-8ba32a0afbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f87ae4-1dfe-5dad-9c1b-8b88e34cb4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f87ae4-1dfe-5dad-9c1b-8b88e34cb4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360566} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b69b343-8f2b-56de-909b-666ce2bb1a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c73664f3-05a3-5a66-826a-ecc745ac4006" :question/math-academy-id "q-162534" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y={2}^{x}$ at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{y}^{″}(x)={2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{[1+{({2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2x}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-1) & =\\frac{{2}^{-1}{ln}^{2}⁡2}{{(1+{2}^{2(-1)}{ln}^{2}⁡2)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{2})}{{(\\frac{4+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}{(4+{ln}^{2}⁡2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a978cfa-f3b8-56ed-a97f-287824704fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118156} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f69f18-5b4e-5c58-8147-8f6771ce39ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118157} {:answer/id #uuid "fd793ef2-0cb9-5d2a-b159-006ba49b52b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118158} {:answer/id #uuid "1217ee08-02cc-5c26-8704-9147c30becfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118159} {:answer/id #uuid "710c75d0-ca77-57a8-adbd-3d60ddd2bdd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a978cfa-f3b8-56ed-a97f-287824704fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118156} :answer-field/id #uuid "a611cfc3-eca3-5620-9f5a-061b630587c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08113b99-b301-5e33-b007-837ba481f7b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34549" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}}|}{{[1+{(\\frac{2}{x-1})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(x-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(x-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2-1)^{2}{[1+\\frac{4}{(2-1)^{2}}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87f9c28c-9a6d-52ed-8c36-f81c165eb93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118318} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae53a6f-1764-5dd8-b68d-438dd15339e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118319} {:answer/id #uuid "ac29bce5-05d3-5d55-a635-325468f76f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118320} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb3de63-fccc-5de1-af36-e34cf5ec2edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118321} {:answer/id #uuid "e188503b-e80a-572e-932b-ecb8e5714e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae53a6f-1764-5dd8-b68d-438dd15339e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118319} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e70090-135d-5be5-8f5f-52c099b16db7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c3279eb-5aad-5467-9349-e9a454f6c445" :question/math-academy-id "q-35190" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+1$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-2x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=12x-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{12x-2}{{[1+{(6{x}^{2}-2x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{12(0)-2}{{[1+{(6\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9777d1a5-c0d1-5e45-a5c5-8804d9a99224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119032} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2e66a9-128e-571e-8b39-e64df411f919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119033} {:answer/id #uuid "1816e76f-b533-5aa6-a5b0-dee6d82320ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119034} {:answer/id #uuid "5c618108-f77b-578b-a3ec-390444c1ccfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119035} {:answer/id #uuid "784bf460-93ab-573f-92f2-1afac72cdaba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1816e76f-b533-5aa6-a5b0-dee6d82320ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119034} :answer-field/id #uuid "c318bce1-18de-5fa1-b45d-da4ab5a7464b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf912f7e-2809-5344-9478-be3125239509" :question/math-academy-id "q-64901" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
